{"id":223792301190476,"type":"song","name":"Spinning Wheel","artist":"Blood, Sweat & Tears","year":1969,"weight":16,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/734ef21049fa84923250d41944db5d06/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"Spinning Wheel (Live)","yt":"SFEewD4EVwU"},"artist_ref":{"id":124235791692623,"name":"Blood, Sweat & Tears","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood%2C_Sweat_%26_Tears.png?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":27,"year":1969,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null},{"id":279209370709843,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1969","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1969,"rank":27,"year":1969,"note":"","total":101,"scaruffi":null},{"id":54802705900031,"name":"Grammy · Song of the Year","src":"singles","grp":"grammy_awards","list_year":null,"rank":60,"year":1970,"note":"nominee","total":371,"scaruffi":null},{"id":62506810646853,"name":"Grammy · Record of the Year","src":"singles","grp":"grammy_awards","list_year":null,"rank":60,"year":1970,"note":"nominee","total":369,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This song was written by David Clayton Thomas two years before he joined Blood, Sweat & Tears, and was initially rejected in his native Canada for being \"too jazzy\" and hard to sell. It became a perfect fit for the band, noted for its \"horns forward jazz\" style, and was one of the biggest hits of 1969. The lyrics discuss letting go of troubles, not conforming, and allude to the 1960s psychedelic movement, without being overly intellectual or vacuous. \"The song Spinning Wheel does not require too much intellectually but it's not completely vacuous either.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-38-the-in